article.scientific_purpose_and_applications:
The main purpose of a lunar GC-MS would be to determine the chemical composition of volatile materials. For planetary surface science this can mean identifying water-derived and carbon-bearing compounds, noble gases and other species that are released when a sample is heated [9][10]. For the Moon, a principal motivation is the possible presence of volatiles in permanently shadowed polar regolith. Interpretation of such measurements is uncertain, and the sources reviewed do not establish the abundance of any particular volatile at a specific lunar location.

Applications discussed in the sources reviewed include:
- analysis of volatiles released from regolith during controlled heating [9][10];
- measurement of the composition of a tenuous atmosphere or exosphere, as an operating mode of a neutral gas mass spectrometer [9];
- analysis of gases from sample handling or processing systems, a common laboratory-style use [5];
- comparison between surface and orbital composition measurements, once instruments of this type are operating [9].

The historical Mars and Titan instruments were designed for atmospheric and soil analyses on other worlds rather than for the Moon. Their results inform the design and interpretation of later instruments but do not establish lunar findings [1][3][7].

article.limitations_and_uncertainties:
The name "gas chromatograph-mass spectrometer" describes an instrument class rather than one design. Values such as mass, power, mass range and mass resolution must always be attributed to a specific instrument and source. This article does not present a single universal specification.

The following claims are not verified against primary sources in this run:
- the Viking GC-MS mass (below 15 kg) and size (about one foot cubed) and its contractors [13];
- the Huygens GCMS mass (17.3 kg), energy budget (110 Wh), mass range (2 to 141 daltons), detection threshold (about 10^-8) and average power (41 W) [3];
- the Luna-Resurs GC mass-resolution and column details [10];
- the Resource Prospector GC-MS content in its volatile-analysis subsystem [12];
- all numerical values for SAM, and its qualitative instrument description, which was drawn from secondary search summaries rather than the primary paper [7].

Other gaps:
- No lunar surface GC-MS flight was found in the sources reviewed. This is not a complete survey of all national and commercial lunar missions.
- The INFICON MSolo instrument mentioned in secondary sources is described by the vendor as a quadrupole mass spectrometer rather than a GC-MS. It is excluded from the GC-MS examples [14].
- The Mars Viking organics question is still under debate, and the reanalysis relies on secondary summaries [11][13].
- Images from the University of Bern page and the Planetary Society page have not been checked for full-resolution downloads or reuse rights.
